<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harsha of Kashmir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> of the Lohara dynasty, reigning from 1089 to 1101. A cultured ruler and poet, he later turned to heavy taxation and melted down temple images to pay his troops, conflicts recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Kalhana's Rajatarangini</a>. Revolts led by the Damara landholders and his kinsmen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uchchala">Uchchala</a> and Sussala overthrew him, and he was killed in 1101.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harsha-of-kashmir.jpg" alt="Harsha of Kashmir" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harshadeva_of_Kashmir_1089-1101_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, also called Harshadeva, was born in 1059, the son of King Kalasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Almost everything known of him comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Rajatarangini</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a>, whose father Champaka had been one of Harsha's ministers and who wrote in the time of Jayasimha, between 1127 and 1159. According to Kalhana he was built like a god and extremely handsome. He is said to have begun as a capable and noble king before his spending brought him into financial trouble.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Extravagant outlay on his troops and on pleasures left the king in grave difficulties. The historian Prithvi Nath Kaul Bamzai notes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a>'s statement that even night soil was taxed. An accidental discovery of hoarded treasure at the Bhimasahi temple led him to despoil other temples and to melt down gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> images. Kalhana writes that not one temple kept its images, and that only two chief divine images were respected, Ranaswamin in the city and Martanda. Two colossal statues of the Buddha were saved by requests made one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parihaspora">Parihasapura</a> by the singer Kanaka and one in the city by the monk Kusalsri.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a> calls him that Turushka, and he bore the epithet raja-Turushka, Turk king. Turkish troops served him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and the chronicle says he continually supported Turushka captains-of-hundreds with money, while adding that he ate domesticated pigs until his death. According to the historian André Wink, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> in all likelihood took up the Muslim tradition of iconoclasm, as the epithet indicates. Kalhana also charges him with credulity towards parasites at court, who passed off slave girls as goddesses, and with incest with his sisters and violence against Naga, his father's sister's daughter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> died on 31 August 1101, the last ruler of the First Lohara dynasty. His conduct has remained a subject of discussion. The great Buddha of the capital outlived him: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a> records that when a fire reduced the city to a heap of earth in the reign of Sussala, the statue alone stood visible, blackened by smoke. The statue at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parihaspora">Parihasapura</a> had been made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitaditya-muktapida">Lalitaditya Muktapida</a>, and Abul-Fazl states that the temples of that place were finally destroyed by Sikandar Butshikan, who ruled from 1389 to 1413.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harsha of Kashmir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King <b>Harsha</b>, who ruled from 1089 to 1101, was a cultured patron but melted down temple images to pay his troops, and was killed in a revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/lohara-dynasty">Lohara dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.5,76&amp;year=1091" aria-label="Kashmir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6481 914 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harsha of Kashmir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6931 1184h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6931 1184h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.5,76&amp;year=1091">What was here in 1091? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
